 Science\nMechanical and Production Engineering\nPhD Committee\nMeet our PhD students\nQuantitative Genetics and Genomics\nMechanical and Production Engineering\nThe Department of Mechanical and Production Engineering PhD programme hosts approx. 28 PhD students. Focus is on applied research and we enjoy strong collaboration with the industry on core issues in all of our four research areas.\nMeet our PhD students\nResearch facilities\nWithin the Mechanical and Production Engineering area, advanced laboratory facilities are available. Graduate students are expected to take part in developing novel equipment.\nCollaboration with the industry also ensures access to state-of-the-art industrial R&D facilities.\nGeographic location\nAU\u2019s localisation strategy aims to ensure that the educations and research activities are placed in the scientific environment to which they are most closely related. The academic environment helps strengthen the growing cluster of technology centered in Aarhus and ensure continued positive commercial development in the region. Most PhD students in Mechanical and Production Engineering are located at Katrinebjerg, Aarhus and not very far from the main campus.\nEmployment after graduation\nMost Mechanical and Production Engineering graduates take up a career in the industry or in consultancy; both are areas where project leadership is important, and this often becomes a stepping stone towards leadership.\nHead of programme\nSubburaj Karupppasamy\nAssociate Professor\nM subburaj@mpe.au.dk\nH 5128 - 140\nP +4587151756\nP +4593521393\nLocal Programme Secretary\nMiriam N\u00f8rs\u00f8ller\nSecretariat Staff Member\nM mirno@mpe.au.dk\nH 5128, 234\nP +4521345479\nGSTS PhD Partner\nAnne Visby Larsen\nPhD Partner\nM avl@au.dk\nH 1521 - 128\nP +4540265495\nRevised\n16.09.2026\n-\nmpe@au.dk\nGraduate School of Technical Sciences\nNy Munkegade 120, building 1521\n8000 Aarhus C.\nSee location on a map\nCVR-nr: 31119103\nEAN-nr: 57 98 000 433 779\nAbou